WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:10.830
“Reskilling” is something that sounds like a buzzword but is actually a requirement if we plan to have a future in which a lot of would-be workers do not get left behind.
“再培训”听起来像个时髦词，但如果我们打算迎来一个不让大批潜在劳动者掉队的未来，它实际上是一项必要条件。

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:19.200
We know we are moving into a period where the jobs in demand will change rapidly, as will the requirements of the jobs that remain.
我们知道，我们正步入这样一个时期：有市场需求的工作岗位将迅速变化，留存下来的岗位，其要求也会如此。

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:28.710
Research by the World Economic Forum finds that on average 42 percent of the “core skills” within job roles will change by 2022.
世界经济论坛的研究发现，到2022年，各岗位的“核心技能”中平均将有42%发生变化。

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:31.150
That is a very short timeline.
这个时限非常短。

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.260
The question of who should pay for reskilling is a thorny one.
再培训应该由谁出钱，这个问题很棘手。

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:45.450
For individual companies, the temptation is always to let go of workers whose skills are no longer in demand and replace them with those whose skills are.
对单个企业来说，总会有这样一种诱惑：辞退技能已不再有市场需求的员工，换上技能有市场需求的人。

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.860
That does not always happen.
这种情况并不总会发生。

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:58.330
AT&T is often given as the gold standard of a company that decided to do a massive reskilling program rather than go with a fire-and-hire strategy.
AT&T常被举为这样一家标杆企业：它决定实施大规模的再培训计划，而不是采用先解雇、再招聘的策略。

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.440
Other companies have also pledged to create their own plans.
其他公司也已承诺制定各自的计划。

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:09.440
When the skills mismatch is in the broader economy, though, the focus usually turns to government to handle it.
不过，技能错配一旦出现在更广泛的经济领域，人们关注的焦点通常就会转向政府，由政府来应对。

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:22.760
Efforts in Canada and elsewhere have been arguably languid at best, and have given us a situation where we frequently hear of employers begging for workers even at times and in regions where unemployment is high.
加拿大和其他地方的努力，往好里说也可以说是有气无力，还让我们陷入了这样一种局面：经常听说雇主苦求人手，即便在失业率高的时期和地区也是如此。

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.920
With the pandemic, unemployment is very high indeed.
疫情之下，失业率的确非常高。

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:39.560
In February, at 3.5 percent and 5.5 percent respectively, unemployment rates in the U.S. and Canada were at generational lows and worker shortages were everywhere.
2月份，美国和加拿大的失业率分别为3.5%和5.5%，处于一代人以来的低点，用工短缺随处可见。

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:51.120
As of May, those rates had spiked up to 13.3 percent and 13.7 percent, and although many worker shortages had disappeared, not all had done so.
截至5月，这两个失业率已飙升至13.3%和13.7%；虽然许多用工短缺已经消失，但并非全都消失了。

00:01:51.920 --> 00:02:01.400
In the medical field, to take an obvious example, the pandemic meant that there were still clear shortages of doctors, nurses and other medical personnel.
举一个明显的例子：在医疗领域，疫情意味着医生、护士和其他医务人员依然明显短缺。

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:08.340
Of course, it is not like you can take an unemployed waiter and train him to be a doctor in a few weeks.
当然，又不是说你可以找来一个失业的服务员，用几周时间就把他培训成医生。

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:16.170
But even if you cannot close the gap, maybe you can close others, and doing so would be to the benefit of all concerned.
但即使填补不了这个缺口，或许也能填补别的缺口，而这样做会让所有相关方受益。

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:30.550
That seems to be the case in Sweden: When forced to furlough 90 percent of their cabin staff, Scandinavian Airlines decided to start up a short retraining program that reskilled the laid-off workers to support hospital staff.
瑞典似乎就是这种情况：北欧航空公司在被迫让90%的客舱乘务人员停薪休假时，决定启动一项短期再培训计划，使这些下岗员工掌握新技能，去协助医院的工作人员。

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:36.840
The effort was a collective one and involved other companies as well as a Swedish university.
这是一次集体行动，参与者还包括其他公司以及瑞典的一所大学。
